### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting that park() can return spuriously. Just like wait(), park() may return without unpark() being called. Always check the condition in a loop.

```java
// WRONG: assuming park() only returns on unpark()
LockSupport.park();
processTask();  // may run even if unpark was not called!

// CORRECT: check condition in a loop
while (!taskReady) {
    LockSupport.park();
}
processTask();
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you use park/unpark to implement a simple mutex? Yes, but it is complex and error-prone. Use ReentrantLock instead, which is built on AQS (which uses park/unpark internally).
- Does park() respond to interrupts? Yes. If the thread is interrupted while parked, park() returns and the interrupt status is set. Check Thread.interrupted() after park().
